1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,071
Welcome.

2
00:00:01,071 --> 00:00:07,326
In the next few minutes you'll learn to use the yard logistics view of the What-If Tool: compare

3
00:00:07,326 --> 00:00:13,125
simulator runs, find where trucks lose time, and let the AI assistant draft improvements.

4
00:00:14,425 --> 00:00:16,796
The tool doesn't simulate the yard.

5
00:00:16,796 --> 00:00:23,095
It reads runs from the yard simulator, compares them side by side, shows where trucks waited,

6
00:00:23,095 --> 00:00:26,075
and lets a local AI draft and discuss fixes.

7
00:00:27,375 --> 00:00:30,580
From the home page, click Load Yard Simulation.

8
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:36,513
At the top is a map of the yard: the check-in terminal at the gate, parking, the scale,

9
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:38,559
and twenty-five storage silos.

10
00:00:38,559 --> 00:00:41,150
Remember the check-in terminal, LT010.

11
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:45,667
The Comparison tab puts every run side by side.

12
00:00:45,667 --> 00:00:47,926
With thirty trucks, nobody waits.

13
00:00:47,926 --> 00:00:51,212
With fifty-two, the longest wait is six minutes.

14
00:00:51,212 --> 00:00:55,662
With seventy-one, it jumps to three hours and thirty-six minutes.

15
00:00:55,662 --> 00:00:58,400
Click Select to star the run you prefer.

16
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,600
Open the WaitingProblem tab.

17
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:08,386
The top bottleneck is off-yard waiting: three trucks queuing outside the gate for one check-in slot.

18
00:01:08,386 --> 00:01:13,475
So the fix is check-in capacity or spacing out arrivals, not a bigger gate.

19
00:01:14,775 --> 00:01:21,445
Click any bottleneck, like LT010, and the chart shows how many trucks were there, minute by minute,

20
00:01:21,445 --> 00:01:24,275
against its capacity: the red dashed line.

21
00:01:25,575 --> 00:01:30,986
Further down, the per-truck timeline gives every truck one row, and red means waiting.

22
00:01:30,986 --> 00:01:32,685
Hover a row for its totals.

23
00:01:32,685 --> 00:01:38,600
This truck waited three hours and thirty-six minutes: eighty-three percent of its whole visit.

24
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:41,577
Open the Improvements tab.

25
00:01:41,577 --> 00:01:44,093
The target run starts on the worst one.

26
00:01:44,093 --> 00:01:49,576
Choose how many proposals you want, from one to five, and click Generate suggestions.

27
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:53,575
The local model takes from about half a minute to two minutes.

28
00:01:54,875 --> 00:02:00,182
Each draft names its target, the changes, the expected impact and the risks.

29
00:02:00,182 --> 00:02:05,629
Check the numbers before you save: this one says LT010 goes from two to three,

30
00:02:05,629 --> 00:02:08,632
but it actually serves one truck at a time.

31
00:02:08,632 --> 00:02:13,450
The tool checks that the places a draft names exist, not its numbers.

32
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:18,138
Click Discuss on a saved proposal to ask about it.

33
00:02:18,138 --> 00:02:22,339
We asked how confident the AI is that it fixes the bottleneck.

34
00:02:22,339 --> 00:02:26,337
It answered moderate to high, citing the real queue scores.

35
00:02:26,337 --> 00:02:29,725
Remember: it explains, but it hasn't run anything.

36
00:02:31,025 --> 00:02:37,342
To recap: open the yard case, compare the runs, find the bottleneck, read the truck timeline,

37
00:02:37,342 --> 00:02:41,350
let the AI draft fixes, and discuss them before you decide.

38
00:02:42,650 --> 00:02:49,208
Once the simulator connection is live, Send to simulator will test a saved proposal for real.

39
00:02:49,208 --> 00:02:52,240
Until then, the AI suggests and you decide.

40
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:53,650
Thanks for watching.
